WARNING: BEFORE RUNNING A CALCULATION FOR PRODUCTION,
TEST THE PSEUDOPOTENTIAL AND BASIS SETS, AND PERFORM
THE CONVERGENCE TESTS (MESH CUTOFF, ETC.) 

IN THE PRESENT EXAMPLES, AND IN ORDER TO SPEED THE CALCULATIONS,
WE PROVIDE SOME VALUES OF THESE PARAMETERS FOR YOU.

WE DO NOT WARRANTY THAT THE VALUES OF THESE PARAMETERS ARE CONVERGED.

THE RESULTS PRESENTED BELOW HAVE BEEN OBTAINED USING:
Version of Siesta: siesta-3.0-b (serial mode)
Compiler: g95 (version 0.92)
Compilation flags: FFLAGS= -O2 -Wall
                   FPPFLAGS= -DGRID_DP $(FPPFLAGS_CDF) $(FPPFLAGS_MPI)
Double precision for the grid variables enabled.
No linear algebra libraries used. 
RESULTS MIGHT DIFFER SLIGHTLY DEPENDING ON THE PLATFORM, COMPILER,
AND COMPILATION FLAGS
*******************************************************************************

All the lines after the $ should be written by the user in the command line.

* Inspect the input files, Si.basis_size.fdf, and examine in detail 
the different input variables. More information can be obtained from 
the Siesta web page (http://www.icmab.es/siesta), following the link 
Documentations, and then Manual.

Pay spetial attention to the tag PAO.BasisSize

******************************************************************************
Run the examples and study the convergence of the structural and energetic
properties as a function of the size of the basis size
******************************************************************************

In this particular example, 
starting from the experimental structure, we are going to 
perform a structural relaxation of the lattice vectors using
the conjugate gradient minimization algorithm.
The optimization of the unit cell vectors is carried out subject
to two constraints:
(i) the position of the  atoms in the unit cell are fixed at the 
high symmetry points, and
(ii) the shear stresses are nullified in order to keep the cell angles
fixed to 60 degrees, characteristic of the fcc lattice.
Regarding this minimization procedure, 
pay particular attention to the input variables:

MD.TypeOfRun       CG               # We are going to perform a 
                                    #   Conjugate Gradient (CG) minimization
MD.VariableCell    .true.           # Is the lattice relaxed together with
                                    #   the atomic coordinates?
MD.NumCGsteps      50               # Number of CG steps for
                                    #   coordinate optimization
MD.MaxStressTol    0.0005 eV/Ang**3 # Tolerance in the maximum
                                    #   stress in a MD.VariableCell CG optimi.
%block GeometryConstraints          # Constraints impossed on 
   position   1   2                 #   the atomic positions 
   stress     4   5   6             #   the shear stresses
%endblock GeometryConstraints

* Run the code for bulk Si with different basis set sizes

$ siesta < Si.sz.fdf  > Si.sz.out
$ siesta < Si.dz.fdf  > Si.dz.out
$ siesta < Si.szp.fdf > Si.szp.out
$ siesta < Si.dzp.fdf > Si.dzp.out

* In the different output files, search for the number of Sankey-type
orbitals included in the basis set:

$ grep "number of Sankey-type orbitals" *out
Si.dz.out:atom: Total number of Sankey-type orbitals:  8
Si.dzp.out:atom: Total number of Sankey-type orbitals: 13
Si.sz.out:atom: Total number of Sankey-type orbitals:  4
Si.szp.out:atom: Total number of Sankey-type orbitals:  9

* Study the PAO.Basis block for the different basis set sizes
(see the .pdf file with the explanation, or study the Siesta User Guide,
also you can study the exercise "Default").

%block PAO.Basis                 # Define Basis set
Si                    2                    # Species label, number of l-shells
 n=3   0   2                         # n, l, Nzeta
   5.007      4.419
   1.000      1.000
 n=3   1   2 P   1                   # n, l, Nzeta, Polarization, NzetaPol
   6.271      5.007
   1.000      1.000
%endblock PAO.Basis

* Now, we are going to study the relaxed structure and the energy

In order to get this, edit the different output files,
and search for:

- The converged relaxed structure:
Those lines (for the DZP basis set) should look like something close to  

outcoor: Relaxed atomic coordinates (fractional):
    0.00000000    0.00000000    0.00000000   1       1  Si
    0.25000000    0.25000000    0.25000000   1       2  Si

outcell: Unit cell vectors (Ang):
        0.000000    2.705266    2.705266
        2.705266    0.000000    2.705266
        2.705266    2.705266    0.000000

outcell: Cell vector modules (Ang)   :    3.825823    3.825823    3.825823
outcell: Cell angles (23,13,12) (deg):     60.0000     60.0000     60.0000
outcell: Cell volume (Ang**3)        :     39.5968

- The Kohn-Sham energy for the converged relaxed structure

siesta: Program's energy decomposition (eV):
siesta: Ebs     =       -75.650988
siesta: Eions   =       380.802124
siesta: Ena     =       115.349306
siesta: Ekin    =        81.916758
siesta: Enl     =        29.311909
siesta: DEna    =         3.716705
siesta: DUscf   =         0.267046
siesta: DUext   =         0.000000
siesta: Exc     =       -65.238641
siesta: eta*DQ  =         0.000000
siesta: Emadel  =         0.000000
siesta: Emeta   =         0.000000
siesta: Emolmec =         0.000000
siesta: Ekinion =         0.000000
siesta: Eharris =      -215.479042
siesta: Etot    =      -215.479041     ---> We are interested in this number
siesta: FreeEng =      -215.479041

* See how the total energy decreases with the number of atomic orbitals in
the basis set (the problem is variational).

* See also how the larger the size the basis sets, the smaller the lattice constant.

